  "readings": [
    "The few-epoch question is read only where every width a primary uses was still improving its validation loss from E2 to E3 (NOT_OVERFIT). The models are small (0.73-34.7 M parameters, depth 4) and see far fewer tokens per parameter than compute-optimal training; \"closer to Jane Street's setting\" means 3 epochs and no detected overfitting, nothing more.",
    "Labels follow Holm's step-down over M, FALL and RISE at family-wise 5% (two-sided t tests, ten draws); every primary's Holm-adjusted p-value is reported.",
    "M: at matched train-validation gap, p* at 768 is below p* at 384 (CAPACITY_BEYOND_MEMORIZATION). This contradicts the memorization-only account, which predicts NOT_DETECTED. It is not by itself evidence of a capacity effect. At matched gap the larger width has had fewer epochs, so a nonzero M is also what p* gives if it depends on training progress rather than on the gap (stated in advance; in the planning fixtures an epoch-only curve read CAPACITY_BEYOND_MEMORIZATION in 96-99% of replicates). The gap includes each width's E0 offset (training articles from WikiText-103, validation from WikiText-2's validation split); mean E0 gap: 384 -0.0341, 768 -0.0350.",
    "FALL: at E3, p* rises with width over 384-768 (CONTINUES_RISING): no turn-down within this ladder. This matches the memorization-only prediction, and also Jane Street's pattern with a peak above 768; the study cannot separate the two.",
    "RISE: at E3, p* rises with width over 64-384 (RISES), as both accounts predict.",
    "Data: WikiText-103 raw training articles, excluding every article equal by title or text to a WikiText-2 article; the tokenizer, the validation set and the pre-training pool are those of the earlier natural-text studies. The common rate was chosen on the tuning draw t00 by validation NLL only."
  ],
